{"kind":"report","text":"CHANGES-REQUESTED for the connectors design note at exact clean head 0178bad99f169a70e1da5e78a2eb0427e5b196c2.\n\nWHAT HOLDS. Refusing a generic two-way mirror is the right top-level shape: observe inbound, render owned surfaces outbound, and put discrete side effects through the work loop. One rostered connector identity with foreign principals carried as data is honest and remains manageable at hundred-person scale; projections should always retain both the connector signer and the claimed principal. The broad durable-versus-ephemeral split is sound: mutable chatter stays ephemeral unless promoted, while charter-selected structural transitions become durable. The note correctly identifies the public-repository doorstep, alias escalation, untrusted text, secret evidence, and the current server-side custody problem for ephemeral speech. Relevant workroom, nexus, service, and app package tests pass. The checkout is exact and clean, git diff --check passes, both commits carry the governing Rests-On trailer, and all path-at-0a66e85 citations were inspected.\n\nMUST FIX 1: the charter is described as having causal and containment force that the current system does not give it. Lines 149-152 say superseding the charter makes everything under the old mapping flare, but the note never requires each connector-derived root act to rest on the live charter and, where relevant, the live alias or admission claim. Without that edge, staleness cannot propagate. Lines 189-195 also say an observation-only connector cannot decide and containment weakens exactly as delegated, but app.go:734 only checks that the actor fingerprint is statically allowlisted; the fold does not consult charter bodies. Any allowlisted connector can state a request and, because it is then requester, ratify a report against that request. Specify the causal basis carried by observations and request chains, how the connector proves the charter and doorstep claim are live before submission, and whether this is policy for an honest connector or an enforced profile boundary. A compromised connector key is not contained by the charter as written.\n\nMUST FIX 2: the claimed delegated authority is broader than the charter scope. Requester-owned report ratification at lines 161-167 is accurate and needs no ratifier role. But the existing role machinery cited at lines 177-180 grants a room-wide ratifier role for every assertion, proposal, and governance statement; it has no repository, team, event-kind, or charter scope. Therefore lines 189-191 cannot say containment weakens only as far as the charter delegates if the connector receives that role. Limit the current automatic design to reports on requests the connector itself filed, or identify scoped ratification as a missing profile/core mechanism. Do not present the global ratifier role as scoped charter delegation.\n\nMUST FIX 3: the symmetric-sync refusal hides the one reconciliation case the connector cannot avoid. Lines 36-41 overstate that reconciliation over an append-only signed log has no honest implementation; a corrective act citing prior observations is honest even though mutation is not. More importantly, commands at lines 64-73 have an ambiguous crash boundary: a foreign side effect can succeed and the connector can die before reporting. Retrying may duplicate a posted comment, while leaving the promise open may hide a completed action. State the required foreign idempotency key, correlation marker plus readback, or durable command/outcome recovery protocol. The never-read-back rule applies safely to connector-owned renderings, not automatically to commands. The no-database claim at lines 284-289 must be conditional on reconstructible correlation or foreign idempotency.\n\nMUST FIX 4: the proposed client-signed frame endpoint is not yet compatible with the cited nexus frame format. The actor signature covers the nexus generation, conversation ID, nexus-assigned sequence, and previous hash. Under concurrent speakers, a client cannot reliably sign those values in one request before the nexus orders the frame. The design needs either a challenge/reservation flow or a detached client-signed payload/intent that the nexus verifies before assigning order and co-signing. It must also say how a live session lease binds the key, how roster membership is checked, and how replay and stale-tip races behave. Because ephemeral chatter is the default inbound path, this belongs in the critical-path design or its open questions, not only as accept a signed envelope.\n\nMUST FIX 5: one citation is still inaccurate after the correction commit. The requester authority sentence cites SKILL.md:53@0a66e85, but the requester-for-report rule is on lines 51-52; line 53 is the continuation about proposals and governance. The other cited paths and lines match 0a66e85.\n\nThe doorstep and alias sections name the right hazards, but a charter gate is adequate only after findings 1 and 2 distinguish honest-connector policy from mechanical authority, causally bind admitted acts to the live charter and alias basis, and avoid treating a foreign alias or a global ratifier grant as narrowly scoped authority.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:83e4037e0ab008d33c905d6de54f42175be773fa","head":"0178bad99f169a70e1da5e78a2eb0427e5b196c2","verdict":"changes-requested"}}